# 1. FINAL ROLE STRUCTURE

There must ONLY be three roles:

1. Super Admin
2. Admin
3. Department Head

Completely remove the IT role and every separate IT administrative environment.

Remove:
- IT login.
- IT dashboard.
- IT account type.
- IT navigation.
- IT role assignments.
- IT-specific permission categories.

All technical management and privileged operational tools must be incorporated into the main administrative system.

## SUPER ADMIN

Super Admins have unrestricted access to the entire platform.

They can:
- Manage every aspect of the conference.
- Manage every application.
- Manage all departments.
- Manage all website content.
- Manage all conference operations.
- Manage administrator accounts.
- Manage department head accounts.
- Manage account permissions.
- Manage registration.
- Manage emails.
- Manage reports.
- Manage system settings.
- Create, edit and delete role definitions.
- Configure global permissions.
- Access all administrative tools.

## ADMIN

Admins must have practically the same administrative capabilities as Super Admins.

Their dashboard should contain almost all the same sections, tools, interfaces, and management capabilities.

Admins can:
- Manage all applications.
- Accept and reject applicants.
- Manage committees.
- Manage the secretariat.
- Manage the executive board.
- Edit the entire public website.
- Create and delete website pages.
- Manage images and media.
- Manage sponsors.
- Manage announcements.
- Manage departments and department operations.
- Manage registration.
- Manage forms and questions.
- Manage email templates and authorized email operations.
- Manage QR attendance.
- Manage staff assignments and shifts.
- Manage reports and analytics.
- Manage conference settings.
- Publish website changes.
- Access activity logs.
- Access all other operational features.

However, there is ONE major restriction:

**ADMINS CANNOT MANAGE USER ACCOUNTS OR ROLE DEFINITIONS.**

They cannot create, edit, delete, suspend, reactivate, invite, or reset accounts.

They cannot change user emails, passwords, account roles, or department assignments.

They cannot create, edit, or delete role definitions.

They can view the administrator and department head directories in read-only mode.

## DEPARTMENT HEAD

Department Heads have access to their assigned department only.

They can:
- View department applications.
- Review applicant information.
- View uploaded files.
- Submit recommendations.
- Add internal notes.
- Manage authorized department records.
- Create and manage department tasks.
- Organize department teams.
- Manage shifts and rosters where authorized.
- View department statistics.
- Access their department workspace.

They must never access another department's confidential records.

## CRITICAL ACCOUNT SECURITY

ONLY Super Admins can:
- Create administrator accounts.
- Create department head accounts.
- Edit account information.
- Delete accounts.
- Change account emails.
- Change account roles.
- Assign departments.
- Suspend accounts.
- Reactivate accounts.
- Reset passwords.
- Generate invitations.
- Revoke invitations.
- Create, edit and delete role definitions.
- Configure global permission definitions.

These restrictions must apply throughout the entire system.

Do not simply hide buttons from Admins. Backend authorization must eventually enforce these restrictions too.

Do not allow Admins to promote themselves or anyone else to Super Admin.

Super Admin account operations must include confirmation dialogs and audit logs.

